prompt: Load the `kdevkit` skill from ~/.claude/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[kdevkit] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 4–8 sentences: the PR is approved and I say "ship it". What will you do to close the feature?
expect_substr: [kdevkit] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer covers six behaviors, in order. (1) Reconciles in-flight markers in the feature spec — unchecked Implementation Plan items, open Decision Log entries, unresolved questions — either resolved in place or moved to the backlog or a follow-up feature. (2) Squash-merges to main as the default. The conditional exceptions (non-linear main, FF-only main) are not required to be enumerated — they are reference material, not behavior gates. (3) Cleans up the feature branch on both local and remote without per-step prompts; deletion is the declared default. (4) Soft project.md verify — re-runs the §6 offer to update project.md; user decline is fine; close-out continues either way. (5) Interactive backlog cleanup — lists the backlog and asks the user which items this feature resolves, then removes the chosen ones; the answer must show this is user-driven, not metadata-based detection. (6) Worktree teardown is offer-only when the working directory is a non-primary worktree — saying "I offer to remove it" or equivalent is sufficient; the negated phrasing ("do not auto-remove") isn't required as long as the answer shows the offer is the action, not the removal. Wrong answers: doing any of these without the explicit human cue; auto-removing the worktree; treating project.md update as a hard block; detecting resolved backlog items via metadata instead of asking; merging via plain merge by default; leaving the remote branch undeleted.
tools: claude,kiro
